<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; background-color: #eaf2f8; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Discovering Nature’s Wonders – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-style: italic; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Nature’s Wonders</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Slow, Furry Sloth!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Make a list of items. Interactive: Write words or phrases as responses to classroom polls or questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can make a list of items related to nature or animals (e.g., "tree, bird, rock"). Can write words or phrases as responses to classroom polls, questions, or cues about animals or nature (e.g., Cue: "slow animal." Response: "sloth." Cue: "colorful bird." Response: "parrot." Poll: "Which animal do you like?" Response: "monkey."). Can write simple phrases or sentences about animals or their environments with guidance (e.g., "The sloth lives in trees.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards of rainforest animals (sloth, toucan, monkey, parrot). The teacher asks, "What animal is it?" Students respond orally. Then, the teacher says, "Let's remember how to write these words!" The teacher points to a written word (e.g., "sloth") on a larger card and asks students to trace the letters in the air with their fingers while saying the word. This activates prior knowledge and sets a writing-focused context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing the word "sloth" on the whiteboard, enunciating /s/ in "sloth." Then, the teacher writes "furry sloth" and "slow sloth" next to it, saying, "The sloth is furry. The sloth is slow." The teacher emphasizes initial sounds for phonemic awareness (e.g., pointing to 's' for 'sloth'). The teacher also writes "rainforest" on the board, enunciating /r/ in "rainforest," and reminds students about the jungle /dʒ/ sound in "jungle."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the sloth fast or slow?" (Expected answer: Slow)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the sloth have fur?" (Expected answer: Yes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large "Animal Fact Card" for a sloth, displayed visually (e.g., on a projector or large poster). The card includes a picture of a sloth and very simple written phrases/a short list:
                    <br><b>"A Sloth"</b>
                    <br><i>- It is slow.</i>
                    <br><i>- It is furry.</i>
                    <br><i>- It lives in trees.</i>
                    <br><i>- It eats leaves.</i>
                    <br><i>- It sleeps a lot.</i>
                    <br>The genre is a simple fact card, register is informational and child-friendly.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Writes simple words or phrases about familiar topics, such as animals or their environment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the fact card aloud, pointing to each word. Then, the teacher asks students to identify and point to specific words on the card:
                </p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the word 'sloth'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Find the word 'slow'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Circle the word 'trees' on your handout (if applicable)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing adjectives + nouns (e.g., slow sloth, furry sloth, colorful toucan), and simple sentences using Present simple (e.g., "It lives in the rainforest."). Vocabulary from the theme will be reinforced: rainforest, jungle, sloth, toucan, monkey, parrot, frog, snake, jaguar, leaf, river, waterfall, vines, nest, branch, hole, forest; live, grow, climb, swing, hide, sleep, rest, fly, dig, eat; slow, furry, colorful, noisy, quiet, wild, tall, leafy, jumpy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Fill-in-the-Blank:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple sentences and a word bank (e.g., "sloth," "slow," "furry," "rainforest," "trees"). Students complete sentences like: "The ____ is ____." (sloth, slow) and "It lives in the ____." (rainforest). The teacher checks answers orally and writes correct answers on the board for students to verify their spelling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Adjective + Noun Writing:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different rainforest animals. For each picture, students are given an adjective and asked to write the full phrase.
                        <br>Example: (Picture of a toucan) Teacher says, "Colorful." Students write: "colorful toucan."
                        <br>Example: (Picture of a snake) Teacher says, "Long." Students write: "long snake."
                        <br>Example: (Picture of a jungle) Teacher says, "Leafy." Students write: "leafy jungle."
                        <br>This reinforces adjectives + nouns and letter correspondence, including J /dʒ/ in "jungle."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Sentence Writing and Peer Check:</b> Students receive a sentence frame: "The ____ is ____ and ____. It lives in the ____." They choose one animal (sloth, monkey, toucan) and two adjectives from a provided list (slow, furry, colorful, noisy, quiet, wild) to complete the sentence. They also choose a place (trees, rainforest, jungle). After writing, students exchange papers with a partner to check if their partner's animal words and adjectives are spelled correctly and make sense. The teacher circulates, offering support and encouraging respectful language when describing animals.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Favorite Animal List" and a simple sentence. Students will create a small drawing and write about their favorite rainforest animal.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> Each student receives a paper with a space for a drawing and two sections for writing: "My Favorite Animal is a ____." and "It is ____ and ____. It lives in the ____."
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose one rainforest animal (e.g., sloth, toucan, monkey, parrot).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They draw their chosen animal in the provided space.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below the drawing, they complete the sentence "My Favorite Animal is a ____." writing the animal's name (e.g., "sloth," "monkey").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, they complete the descriptive sentence: "It is ____ and ____. It lives in the ____." using adjectives (slow, furry, colorful, noisy, quiet, wild) and environment words (trees, rainforest, jungle).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages them to talk about their preferences ("I like sloths. They are quiet.") and use respectful language for nature ("The sloth is quiet.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in writing simple words and phrases, completing the task with chosen animals and descriptors, and applying target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the production task and review of the students' "My Favorite Animal" writing. A simple checklist can be used to note each student's progress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The students' completed "My Favorite Animal List" with the drawing and descriptive sentences from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write the name of an animal.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least one target adjective (e.g., "slow," "furry," "colorful").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes where the animal lives (e.g., "trees," "rainforest").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility of writing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to apply initial sound/letter correspondence (e.g. 's' for sloth, 'j' for jungle).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write the name of a rainforest animal: ____."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write a word that tells about an animal: ____."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new word did you write today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully wrote animal names and at least one adjective. The guided sentence writing was effective for providing structure. Some students needed more individual support with spelling "rainforest" or specific adjectives like "colorful." I will ensure more visual aids for tricky words in future writing tasks. The peer-checking activity encouraged collaboration and self-correction. What worked well was the multi-sensory approach (tracing, writing, saying). Adjustments for next lesson include providing small word banks with pictures for more independent writing and more practice with common sentence structures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This writing lesson prepares students for Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), where they will consolidate information and create a project. The ability to write simple lists and descriptive sentences will be crucial for summarizing information about rainforest animals and presenting their 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>